---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import TopicClusterNav from '../components/TopicClusterNav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
const breadcrumbs = [{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'About', url: '/about' }];
---
<BaseLayout title="About Our Formulas and Assumptions | Rock Coverage Calculator" description="Understand how Rock Coverage Calculator estimates volume, weight, and bags, where supplier assumptions matter, and the limits of a material quantity estimate." canonicalPath="/about">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'AboutPage',
      name: 'About Rock Coverage Calculator', url: 'https://rockcoveragecalculator.com/about',
      description: 'Formulas, material assumptions, and limitations of the quantity calculators.',
      mainEntity: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Rock Coverage Calculator', url: 'https://rockcoveragecalculator.com' }
    })} />
  </Fragment>
  <Header />
  <main id="main-content">
    <PageHero title="About Rock Coverage Calculator" highlightWord="Calculator" badge="Formulas and assumptions" subtitle="Practical tools for estimating rock, gravel, and stone quantities from your measurements." breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} />
    <article class="about-content container">
      <section aria-labelledby="purpose-title">
        <h2 id="purpose-title">From a measured space to a material order</h2>
        <p>Rock Coverage Calculator helps homeowners and contractors estimate cubic yards, US short tons, and bags for landscape beds, paths, and other aggregate layers. Start with a measured area and the planned layer depth, then check the result against the product your supplier actually sells.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/">main calculator</a> supports several area shapes. The <a href="/calculators">calculator library</a> groups material tools, unit conversions, and project guides so you can choose the estimate that matches the job.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="engineering-standards" aria-labelledby="method-title">
        <h2 id="method-title">How the quantity estimates work</h2>
        <div class="table-scroll"><table>
          <caption>Core conversions for an even layer</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Calculation</th><th scope="col">Assumption to check</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Cubic feet</th><td>Area in square feet × depth in inches ÷ 12</td><td>Measured footprint and finished layer thickness</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Cubic yards</th><td>Cubic feet ÷ 27</td><td>Whether the supplier quotes loose or installed volume</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">US short tons</th><td>Cubic yards × short tons per cubic yard</td><td>The product's loose bulk weight; 1 short ton = 2,000 lb</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Whole bags</th><td>Round up: required cubic feet ÷ labeled cubic feet per bag</td><td>The bag's actual volume; weight-labeled bags also need a density assumption</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Extra material allowances increase the calculated order quantity. They are planning choices, not universal requirements. Some tools also estimate compacted layers; check that tool's stated factor and keep loose ordering volume separate from finished volume.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="density-title">
        <h2 id="density-title">Default densities are planning values</h2>
        <p>Rock weight varies with the source material, particle sizes, voids, packing, and moisture. A default is not a certified measurement of your supplier's stockpile. Where a tool offers a density input, replace its default with the supplier's loose bulk weight.</p>
        <p>Material standards describe specified products or test methods; naming a standard does not certify an online estimate or establish one universal density. Check the <a href="/rock-density-chart">density reference</a> and the assumptions and sources on the individual material page.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="limits-title">
        <h2 id="limits-title">What a quantity calculator can tell you</h2>
        <p>An estimate can help compare order sizes and check unit conversions. It cannot determine the correct structural design, drainage capacity, excavation, or installation specification for your site. Soil, slopes, loading, and local requirements can change the necessary build.</p>
        <p>Calculate distinct layers separately. Confirm stone size, required thickness, compaction, truck capacity, delivery access, and purchasing increments with your supplier or installer before ordering. Use the <a href="/layered-project-calculator">layered project planner</a> when your project includes separate materials.</p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="corrections-title">
        <h2 id="corrections-title">Questions and corrections</h2>
        <p>If an estimate seems wrong, send the page address, measurements, units, and selected assumptions through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. Supplier product links and bag labels help make a correction specific and verifiable.</p>
        <p>Read the <a href="/terms">terms of use</a> and <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
    <TopicClusterNav currentPage="/about" />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .about-content { max-width: 900px; padding-block: var(--space-12); }
  .about-content section { margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }
  .about-content h2 { color: var(--color-text-heading); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .about-content p { line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .about-content a { text-decoration: underline; }
  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; margin-block: var(--space-5); }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; }
  caption { text-align: left; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  th, td { padding: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); vertical-align: top; }
</style>
